gs the eponym span and not the parenthetical descriptor.\n\nActually, the real cause seems linguistic: the model treats \"telangiectasis\" as a disease-naming noun itself, while gold reserves that for cases presented as an independent condition rather than a descriptor of the same disease already mentioned elsewhere (consistent with the title also skipping it). This suggests the pattern generalizes to pathological-finding nouns like those ending in \"-ectasis\" or \"-itis\" that name a structural lesion — similar to how \"exudative retinal detachment\" got annotated as DiseaseClass since it functions as a standalone condition label rather than a synonym.\n\nSo I'm framing this as: TPs for SpecificDisease are genuine nosological names (eponyms, named syndromes, acronyms), while generic pathology descriptors—even in a \"so-called\" parenthetical gloss—get DiseaseClass or nothing, since they describe a mechanism/finding rather than naming the disease itself. I want to propose a linguistic rule: only tag SpecificDisease when the span is a true disease name that could substitute for \"Coats disease\" without changing the referent, and flag the borderline case where \"Coats telangiectasis\" is used elsewhere as an alias, which complicates a clean rule. I'm now drafting the final structured output with a short Step 1 analysis followed by exactly one Pattern Insight entry covering the pattern name, confusion trigger, contrastive evidence, and the proposed linguistic rule, keeping it concise per the requested format.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The True Positive SpecificDisease spans are names of disease entities: eponyms (\"Coats disease,\" \"Norrie disease\"), named syndromes (\"Sjogren-Larsson syndrome\"), and acronyms (\"SLS\"). The discrepant span, \"retinal telangiectasis,\" describes a vascular abnormality, a physical lesion or feature of the disease. The gold schema treats such descriptors differently: \"exudative retinal detachment\" and \"mental retardation\" are both DiseaseClass, not SpecificDisease.\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The span is a disease-like pathology noun (\"-ectasis,\" meaning vessel dilation) with an anatomical modifier (\"retinal\"). It sits in a parenthetical \"so-called\" gloss, which makes it look like an alias for a disease. The model reads the condition-sounding noun phrase plus the alias framing as a named disease.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** The trigger explains this case. The same document supports the gold behavior: the title's \"(unilateral retinal telangiectasis)\" and the abstract's \"Coats telangiectasis\" are also unannotated, so the gold standard consistently reserves the disease label for \"Coats disease.\" This case is borderline, since the abstract does use \"Coats telangiectasis\" as a near-synonym. Also, the record lists Gold as SpecificDisease, but the matched annotations contain no gold span at this offset. This may be a span-alignment problem, but the linguistic error is the same either way: the model over-typed a finding as a named disease.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Formulation**\n\n- **Pattern Insight: Generic Pathological Descriptor Mistaken for a Named Disease**\n  - **Pattern Name:** Lesion/Finding Descriptor in a Parenthetical Gloss Typed as SpecificDisease\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** A disease-like pathology noun phrase (\"retinal telangiectasis,\" where \"-ectasis\" denotes vessel dilation) introduced through a parenthetical \"so-called\" gloss. Its disease-sounding morphology and alias framing lead the model to assign SpecificDisease.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** True Positives tag only nosological names: eponyms, named syndromes, and acronyms that refer to the disease as a whole (\"Coats disease,\" \"Sjogren-Larsson syndrome,\" \"SLS\"). Generic condition or finding descriptors in the same schema (\"exudative retinal detachment,\" \"mental retardation\") fall under DiseaseClass.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Assign SpecificDisease only when the span is a nosological name (an eponym, a proper-noun disease or syndrome name, or an acronym for one) that refers to the disease entity itself. If the span is a descriptive pathological noun phrase (lesion, sign, or feature, such as \"X-ectasis,\" \"X-detachment,\" or \"X-retardation\") denoting a manifestation or mechanism, do not tag it as SpecificDisease, even inside a \"so-called,\" \"also known as,\" or parenthetical gloss. Use DiseaseClass if it names a generic condition, and otherwise leave it untagged.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 3838, "output_tokens": 5950, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 4860}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}